description:
crate-jmap-server/src/helpers.rs:163-187 — the SystemTime-to-(secs, millis) conversion is a 3-level nested match (duration_since result → try_from result → checked_neg result), with defensive sentinels at every layer. The comments are accurate but dense:

  // Negate after the fallible widen so we don't underflow at i64::MIN.
  // .checked_neg() returns None only for i64::MIN, which try_from cannot
  // produce (its output range is [0, i64::MAX]). The branch is therefore
  // unreachable on valid u64 → i64 widening; the .unwrap_or path falls
  // through to the sentinel for defence in depth.

A reader has to internalize three layers of "this branch is technically unreachable but kept for defence" before getting to the actual seconds/millis computation. The control flow would be easier to follow as a small helper:

  fn signed_seconds_since_epoch(now: SystemTime) -> Option<(i64, u32)> {
      match now.duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH) {
          Ok(d) => i64::try_from(d.as_secs()).ok().map(|s| (s, d.subsec_millis())),
          Err(e) => {
              let d = e.duration();
              i64::try_from(d.as_secs())
                  .ok()
                  .and_then(|s| s.checked_neg())
                  .map(|neg| (neg, d.subsec_millis()))
          }
      }
  }

Then now_utc_string becomes:

  let (secs, millis) = signed_seconds_since_epoch(SystemTime::now())
      .unwrap_or_else(|| return UTCDate::from("clock-out-of-range"));

(Or use ? with an early return.)

Smaller cognitive footprint. The defence-in-depth comments stay attached to the helper, not the consumer.

Opinion-leaning; safe to label "opinion" if it doesn't survive a second pair of eyes.
